00:04:03.240 --> 00:04:04.530
So has that been tough for you?

00:04:04.530 --> 00:04:06.180
'Cause recently this happened with my son.

00:04:06.180 --> 00:04:08.130
He didn't, he was like, I was like, wait

00:04:08.130 --> 00:04:09.750
are we not gonna snuggle like before bed?

00:04:09.750 --> 00:04:10.890
And he was like, no.

00:04:10.890 --> 00:04:13.080
And I was like, (whimpers) it's fine.

00:04:13.080 --> 00:04:14.430
I was like, it's totally fine.

00:04:14.430 --> 00:04:16.110
They just stop all a sudden.

00:04:16.110 --> 00:04:18.450
- Yes. And it starts with breastfeeding.

00:04:18.450 --> 00:04:21.930
Like my son at nine months
just stopped breastfeeding

00:04:21.930 --> 00:04:22.763
and I remember just being like.

00:04:22.763 --> 00:04:24.210
- Why don't you want me?

00:04:24.210 --> 00:04:26.400
- Well, not only did I
feel totally rejected,

00:04:26.400 --> 00:04:30.150
but I remember regretting
years later, I was like,

00:04:30.150 --> 00:04:33.300
oh my God, I don't remember
the last time I breastfed him.

00:04:33.300 --> 00:04:36.450
And so with Bella I was
constantly, you know,

00:04:36.450 --> 00:04:37.552
every time I breastfed I was,

00:04:37.552 --> 00:04:41.370
I was like just taking in the moment.

00:04:41.370 --> 00:04:43.380
My baby, this has been
the most rewarding thing

00:04:43.380 --> 00:04:45.480
I've ever done is breastfeed you, my baby.

00:04:45.480 --> 00:04:47.400
- Meanwhile she's like, my mom is a freak.

00:04:47.400 --> 00:04:49.508
She's like, like what's happening?

00:04:49.508 --> 00:04:51.420
- She lasted for two years.

00:04:51.420 --> 00:04:54.420
And I do remember the
last time I breastfed her.

00:04:54.420 --> 00:04:55.860
We were in a mommy and me class

00:04:55.860 --> 00:04:59.010
and there was a loud sound
and she walked over to me.

00:04:59.010 --> 00:05:01.320
She was two, and she said, "Mama, chichi."

00:05:01.320 --> 00:05:04.650
Which is what she adoringly
called my breasts.

00:05:04.650 --> 00:05:09.180
And, and I breastfed her
and she never asked again.

00:05:09.180 --> 00:05:11.280
- Just on her own, just
never asked again? Yeah.

00:05:11.280 --> 00:05:13.680
You're lucky though, 'cause
a lot of women, like my self,

00:05:13.680 --> 00:05:16.800
I had like a, I have like a
couple like autoimmune things.

00:05:16.800 --> 00:05:18.870
This is what I think
hindered me being able to,

00:05:18.870 --> 00:05:20.250
but I could only do it for so long.

00:05:20.250 --> 00:05:22.890
And then it just like, whoop!
Dried up. Desert. Yeah.

00:05:22.890 --> 00:05:23.838
- I mean that happens.

00:05:23.838 --> 00:05:24.960
You know, we all do

00:05:24.960 --> 00:05:26.430
the best that we can.
- That's awesome, yeah.

00:05:26.430 --> 00:05:29.157
I know, I was just like,
are you done? Alright.

00:05:29.157 --> 00:05:30.210
- That's okay.

00:05:30.210 --> 00:05:31.920
- They got all the good stuff,

00:05:31.920 --> 00:05:33.780
but I was just like, I, yeah.

00:05:33.780 --> 00:05:35.610
You're lucky that that happened for you.

00:05:35.610 --> 00:05:37.110
- Yeah, but don't feel bad.

00:05:37.110 --> 00:05:38.760
- I didn't honestly, it hurt,

00:05:38.760 --> 00:05:41.160
and I was really celebrating it.

00:05:41.160 --> 00:05:42.420
- Right. Yeah.

00:05:42.420 --> 00:05:44.580
I don't think moms should
ever feel bad about,

00:05:44.580 --> 00:05:46.073
we're all trying to do
the best that we can.

00:05:46.073 --> 00:05:47.580
- And all our bodies are different.

00:05:47.580 --> 00:05:49.080
- All of our bodies are different.

00:05:49.080 --> 00:05:51.263
What baby needs is different.
- But that's incredible.

00:05:51.263 --> 00:05:52.920
Just scientifically, it's not,

00:05:52.920 --> 00:05:55.020
they've done studies
on how that, you know,

00:05:55.020 --> 00:05:56.820
like how it helps kids and how, you know,

00:05:56.820 --> 00:05:57.780
that's really cool.

00:05:57.780 --> 00:05:59.549
I love that for you.

00:05:59.549 --> 00:06:01.080
So you're celebrating a major milestone.

00:06:01.080 --> 00:06:02.780
It's been 20 years since you started

00:06:02.780 --> 00:06:04.530
as an ambassador for UNICEF.

00:06:04.530 --> 00:06:05.610
That's incredible.

00:06:05.610 --> 00:06:07.949
How did you end up, 20
years is a long time.

00:06:07.949 --> 00:06:10.530
Was there something that
happened in your life

00:06:10.530 --> 00:06:12.480
that made you wanna be
a part? Like why UNICEF?

00:06:12.480 --> 00:06:15.151
- Yeah. Well, in 2000,
I lived in South Africa

00:06:15.151 --> 00:06:17.700
for three months and I was filming--

00:06:17.700 --> 00:06:18.600
- Wait, why? Oh, you were filming.

00:06:18.600 --> 00:06:19.433
- I was filming,

00:06:19.433 --> 00:06:21.660
but I had a very small
part in this in this movie.

00:06:21.660 --> 00:06:24.544
So I would, I would go
volunteer in like townships

00:06:24.544 --> 00:06:26.040
and children's hospitals.

00:06:26.040 --> 00:06:27.630
And when I got back to LA,

00:06:27.630 --> 00:06:29.785
I had such a hard time assimilating

00:06:29.785 --> 00:06:33.990
the person that I discovered
there, which was this

00:06:33.990 --> 00:06:38.520
you know, very empathetic, compassionate--

00:06:38.520 --> 00:06:40.110
- And you can't unsee certain things.

00:06:40.110 --> 00:06:42.150
- Person of service.

00:06:42.150 --> 00:06:45.774
I realized that serving other
people is what fulfilled me.

00:06:45.774 --> 00:06:48.990
And when I came back, I went
through a really hard time

00:06:48.990 --> 00:06:51.570
of depression because I
was like on "Charmed",

00:06:51.570 --> 00:06:52.953
living in a house in Beverly Hills,

00:06:52.953 --> 00:06:55.200
you know, driving a BMW

00:06:55.200 --> 00:06:57.960
and I had just seen such hardship

00:06:57.960 --> 00:07:00.000
and there was a lot of guilt in that.

00:07:00.000 --> 00:07:01.620
And my mom said to me,

00:07:01.620 --> 00:07:06.510
because she was a a huge fan
of UNICEF's lifesaving work,

00:07:06.510 --> 00:07:09.450
and did trick or treat for
UNICEF when she was a kid,

00:07:09.450 --> 00:07:10.470
and then I did it.

00:07:10.470 --> 00:07:12.270
She said, "you should call UNICEF."

00:07:12.270 --> 00:07:16.950
And so in 2003 they, they
appointed me as an ambassador

00:07:16.950 --> 00:07:19.240
and I've been able to
travel to Angola, Africa.

00:07:19.240 --> 00:07:22.320
Two years after the
peace treaty was signed

00:07:22.320 --> 00:07:27.180
for the longest civil war
in the history of the world.

00:07:27.180 --> 00:07:29.910
India, six months after the tsunami,

00:07:29.910 --> 00:07:33.330
to see how UNICEF was
helping communities rebuild.

00:07:33.330 --> 00:07:35.913
Uh, Kosovo, where I learned about,

00:07:37.050 --> 00:07:41.820
really, the disparity in
income and how poverty

00:07:41.820 --> 00:07:45.750
impacts a child's development and future.

00:07:45.750 --> 00:07:46.947
- Obviously, yeah.

00:07:46.947 --> 00:07:49.500
- And then recently I went to Egypt

00:07:49.500 --> 00:07:52.221
where my focus was on young girls

00:07:52.221 --> 00:07:57.210
and there's still some,
some practices in Egypt

00:07:57.210 --> 00:08:00.349
like female genital
mutilation that UNICEF,

00:08:00.349 --> 00:08:05.349
with with the government, is
really trying very hard to ban.

00:08:06.210 --> 00:08:10.009
So, so it's really just very fulfilling.

00:08:10.009 --> 00:08:13.380
Hard work, but important work.

00:08:13.380 --> 00:08:15.000
And I feel very blessed

00:08:15.000 --> 00:08:19.140
that I am still able to
be a person of service.

00:08:19.140 --> 00:08:21.300
- Yeah. I mean it's, it's the
most incredible thing, right?

00:08:21.300 --> 00:08:23.040
'Cause you're super blessed in your life

00:08:23.040 --> 00:08:23.970
and you get these opportunities.

00:08:23.970 --> 00:08:26.850
But to use that spotlight
and for someone that might,

00:08:26.850 --> 00:08:27.817
you know, like, "Oh I love her,

00:08:27.817 --> 00:08:29.490
"and oh my God, she's into
that, what does that mean?"

00:08:29.490 --> 00:08:30.323
Like, you know what I'm saying?

00:08:30.323 --> 00:08:32.370
It is a very cool thing
to use that spotlight.

00:08:32.370 --> 00:08:33.390
So thank you for doing that.

00:08:33.390 --> 00:08:34.380
Do you think, 'cause I feel like

00:08:34.380 --> 00:08:36.720
you do get political in a good way.

00:08:36.720 --> 00:08:38.610
Do you feel like you'd
ever get into politics?

00:08:38.610 --> 00:08:39.570
Would you ever do that?

00:08:39.570 --> 00:08:42.660
- You know, I think it is something,

00:08:42.660 --> 00:08:47.040
if I can figure out a
way to run for office

00:08:47.040 --> 00:08:50.610
and still maintain my integrity.

00:08:50.610 --> 00:08:54.570
Which I'm not sure is, is
something that you can do.

00:08:54.570 --> 00:08:55.540
I think think that,

00:08:55.540 --> 00:08:57.026
I think people--
- That's the saddest statement

00:08:57.026 --> 00:08:57.859
but so true, yeah.

00:08:57.859 --> 00:09:00.510
- I think that our politicians get into it

00:09:00.510 --> 00:09:03.900
for the right reasons and on both sides

00:09:03.900 --> 00:09:06.483
get very caught up in the power of it.

00:09:07.830 --> 00:09:10.200
- Or even how to get out
there. Like to have support.

00:09:10.200 --> 00:09:11.820
- It's such a bureaucracy.

00:09:11.820 --> 00:09:13.950
And they're constantly fundraising

00:09:13.950 --> 00:09:15.570
and they go where the money goes

00:09:15.570 --> 00:09:17.605
and it's just very easy to get,

00:09:17.605 --> 00:09:19.953
I think, caught up in all of that.

00:09:21.000 --> 00:09:21.960
I'm an activist.

00:09:21.960 --> 00:09:23.640
I'm happy to be an activist

00:09:23.640 --> 00:09:25.740
and I feel like I get a lot done.

00:09:25.740 --> 00:09:28.050
If at some point I could figure out

00:09:28.050 --> 00:09:31.171
how to get as much done
and run for office.

00:09:31.171 --> 00:09:33.675
I'm not saying no.
- Against the idea.

00:09:33.675 --> 00:09:36.180
- And also my kids are little.

00:09:36.180 --> 00:09:37.800
And you have to be in DC so much.

00:09:37.800 --> 00:09:39.660
So maybe as they get older.

00:09:39.660 --> 00:09:41.580
- Well everybody who's running for office

00:09:41.580 --> 00:09:43.020
if you ever do this,
they need a good motto.

00:09:43.020 --> 00:09:44.817
And I love, this quote inspires you,

00:09:44.817 --> 00:09:46.260
and I love this quote,
I've heard it before.

00:09:46.260 --> 00:09:47.730
But if you haven't, it's great.

00:09:47.730 --> 00:09:49.338
It says "They tried to bury us,

00:09:49.338 --> 00:09:51.750
"but they didn't know we were seeds."

00:09:51.750 --> 00:09:53.010
Which I think is so powerful.

00:09:53.010 --> 00:09:53.843
What does that say to you?

00:09:53.843 --> 00:09:55.697
I've heard this before and I love it.

00:09:55.697 --> 00:09:58.500
- For me, I feel like personally,

00:09:58.500 --> 00:10:01.080
because I get attacked by so many trolls.

00:10:01.080 --> 00:10:03.528
And they've tried to
cancel me so many times,

00:10:03.528 --> 00:10:08.528
that every time I, they,
they try to cancel me,

00:10:09.090 --> 00:10:11.640
I feel like I grow back stronger.

00:10:11.640 --> 00:10:14.075
And also they're spreading my message

00:10:14.075 --> 00:10:16.890
by talking about it.
- By talking about it.

00:10:16.890 --> 00:10:18.660
- So that's what it means to me.

00:10:18.660 --> 00:10:22.470
But I think, I think it's a
beautiful thing, you know?

00:10:22.470 --> 00:10:24.990
Especially for, you know,

00:10:24.990 --> 00:10:27.900
to teach kids when when things are hard

00:10:27.900 --> 00:10:30.368
and people are mean or whatever.

00:10:30.368 --> 00:10:34.530
Or when people are mean, just
think of yourself as a seed.

00:10:34.530 --> 00:10:37.170
You are, they're planting
a seed for you to grow.

00:10:37.170 --> 00:10:40.560
And I think that's what
activism is mainly, anyway.

00:10:40.560 --> 00:10:43.680
Right? Like I think
about all these activists

00:10:43.680 --> 00:10:47.070
that didn't get to see certain
things in their lifetime.

00:10:47.070 --> 00:10:50.430
Like Alice Paul who wrote
the Equal Rights Amendment,

00:10:50.430 --> 00:10:54.533
which is like my grave, my grave tombstone

00:10:54.533 --> 00:10:55.500
mission.
- What you want on there.

00:10:55.500 --> 00:10:59.070
- It's the thing that I need
to accomplish in my life.

00:10:59.070 --> 00:11:02.430
But she wrote it a
hundred years ago. Right?

00:11:02.430 --> 00:11:04.800
And we're still fighting for the ERA,

00:11:04.800 --> 00:11:06.813
which is basically women being included

00:11:06.813 --> 00:11:11.280
in the constitution or
named in the constitution.

00:11:11.280 --> 00:11:12.240
Right now, women,

00:11:12.240 --> 00:11:14.790
85% of people do not realize that women

00:11:14.790 --> 00:11:17.460
are not included in the constitution.

00:11:17.460 --> 00:11:20.640
We are the only industrialized nation.

00:11:20.640 --> 00:11:22.590
Which is so--
- Which is so crazy.

00:11:22.590 --> 00:11:25.140
'Cause we're known as
possibly the progressive one.

00:11:25.140 --> 00:11:26.089
- But I, yeah.

00:11:26.089 --> 00:11:29.430
I look at Alice Paul and I think, wow.

00:11:29.430 --> 00:11:30.630
- A hundred years later, still.

00:11:30.630 --> 00:11:31.500
- A hundred years later.

00:11:31.500 --> 00:11:32.580
And she didn't get to see

00:11:32.580 --> 00:11:35.100
all that hard work come into fruition.

00:11:35.100 --> 00:11:37.980
How can I be okay with my hard work

00:11:37.980 --> 00:11:39.900
not coming into fruition?

00:11:39.900 --> 00:11:42.120
And at a certain point, you have to be

00:11:42.120 --> 00:11:45.834
just so in love with the
process of trying to make change

00:11:45.834 --> 00:11:49.289
that you realize you're planting the seed.

00:11:49.289 --> 00:11:51.865
You're planting the seed
that hopefully will grow.

00:11:51.865 --> 00:11:53.190
- I think that's the
thing. It's not overnight.

00:11:53.190 --> 00:11:54.750
It's not overnight. So
that's the thing, too.

00:11:54.750 --> 00:11:55.950
Well especially for her. But I just mean

00:11:55.950 --> 00:11:57.107
it's not overnight.
- Especially for women.

00:11:57.107 --> 00:11:58.920
- People wanna see it the next day

00:11:58.920 --> 00:12:01.020
and it's like, that's
not it. It's a grind.

00:12:01.020 --> 00:12:03.510
Like anytime you want change,
it's gonna be a grind.

00:12:03.510 --> 00:12:04.950
And you might not even see it.

00:12:04.950 --> 00:12:07.350
But like we have kids,
you want that for them.

00:12:07.350 --> 00:12:08.970
I want these rights for them.

00:12:08.970 --> 00:12:09.803
You know?

00:12:09.803 --> 00:12:11.880
So I think that's where we
need to start thinking like,

00:12:11.880 --> 00:12:13.530
it's not just about your direct impact.

00:12:13.530 --> 00:12:15.450
It's about everyone's direct impact.

00:12:15.450 --> 00:12:16.650
And what's helpful for all of us.

00:12:16.650 --> 00:12:19.500
- Well and also kids
and young people today

00:12:19.500 --> 00:12:21.030
are the generation where everything

00:12:21.030 --> 00:12:22.953
is immediate gratification, right?

00:12:22.953 --> 00:12:25.117
They could push a button
and it shows up to

00:12:25.117 --> 00:12:25.950
your house the next day.
- Oh my God.

00:12:25.950 --> 00:12:27.660
you just described my daughter, yeah.

00:12:27.660 --> 00:12:29.567
- Right? Like the addiction of it.

00:12:29.567 --> 00:12:31.740
And it starts at a very young age.

00:12:31.740 --> 00:12:33.570
And I think kids' mental health

00:12:33.570 --> 00:12:36.822
is actually really taking
a hit because of it.

00:12:36.822 --> 00:12:37.980
- That's why it should be included

00:12:37.980 --> 00:12:39.090
in the curriculum in schools.

00:12:39.090 --> 00:12:40.680
- I agree!
- Yeah, I know.

00:12:40.680 --> 00:12:41.513
I talk about it all the time.

00:12:41.513 --> 00:12:43.290
People are annoyed with it, probably.

00:12:43.290 --> 00:12:44.400
We need another break,

00:12:44.400 --> 00:12:46.950
but go online to learn more
about UNICEF, everybody.

00:12:46.950 --> 00:12:48.510
And also I want to mention that Alyssa

00:12:48.510 --> 00:12:50.460
has a new movie coming out.

00:12:50.460 --> 00:12:52.140
It's called "Who Are You People?"

00:12:52.140 --> 00:12:53.910
It's in theaters, great title,

00:12:53.910 --> 00:12:56.133
and on demand starting February 24th.

